# Gitmoji Commit Reference

Always start commits with **one emoji** that represents the intent of the change.

## Core Changes

- ✨ feat — introduce a new feature
- 🐛 fix — fix a bug
- 🚑 hotfix — critical fix for production
- 🚀 perf — improve performance
- ♻️ refactor — refactor code without changing behavior
- 🔧 config — change configuration files
- 🔥 remove — remove code or files

## Code Quality & Maintenance

- 🎨 style — formatting, linting, no logic change
- 🧹 chore — maintenance tasks, tooling, dependencies
- ⚡ perf — optimize speed or efficiency
- 🚨 lint — fix compiler or linter warnings

## Documentation & Examples

- 📝 docs — documentation only changes
- 💡 comments — add or update code comments
- 📖 docs — extended documentation or guides

## Testing

- ✅ test — add, update, or fix tests
- 🧪 test — experimental or exploratory tests

## Dependencies & Build

- ⬆️ deps — upgrade dependencies
- ⬇️ deps — downgrade dependencies
- 📦 build — build system or external dependencies
- 👷 ci — CI/CD configuration changes

## UI & Assets

- 💄 ui — UI or styling updates
- 🎨 assets — images, icons, or static resources
- ♿ a11y — accessibility improvements

## Security & Data

- 🔒 security — fix security issues
- 🔐 secrets — manage secrets or credentials
- 🗃️ data — database or data-related changes

## Reverts & Misc

- ⏪ revert — revert a previous commit
- 🏷️ release — versioning or releases
- 🚧 wip — work in progress (avoid if possible)

## Commit Format

<emoji> <type>: <short, present-tense description>

Example:
✨ feat: add password reset flow
